## Learning objectives

By the end of the project, your team should be able to:

1. Document a dataset’s provenance, including amplitude units, sampling rate, montage, reference, event codes and experimental protocol (00, 00a, 01).
2. Justify every preprocessing choice with a figure or a measurement rather than a default (03, 04, 05).
3. Turn epochs into features that match the physiology: ERP amplitudes for evoked paradigms and log band power for rhythm paradigms (02, 06).
4. Train a linear classifier and report chance-corrected performance with grouped, leakage-free cross-validation (02, 06).
5. Test a hypothesis with a controlled comparison where only one factor changes at a time.
6. Explain what the result does and does not show, including its limits as an offline analysis.

## Scope: what you may and may not use

The core pipeline must use only methods from notebooks 00–06. Depth beats breadth: push these tools hard rather than reaching for later ones.

| Stage | In scope (core pipeline) | Out of scope for the core result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Paradigm | P300 / ERP oddball, motor imagery or motor execution EEG | SSVEP, fNIRS, invasive recordings |
| Data handling | MNE Raw/Epochs/Evoked, MOABB or `mne.datasets` loaders, annotations, metadata | Hand-written file parsers that skip MNE objects |
| Preprocessing | FIR/IIR band-pass and notch filters, resampling, re-referencing, EOG regression, ICA, bad-channel marking, baseline correction, amplitude rejection | Source localization, ASR, custom adaptive filters |
| Features | ERP amplitudes in time windows, decimated waveforms, Welch PSD, log band power, channel/band selection | CSP, Riemannian geometry, time-frequency wavelets, Xdawn, CCA |
| Classifiers | LDA (including shrinkage), logistic regression (with regularization) | Neural networks, SVM ensembles, gradient boosting |
| Evaluation | Grouped CV (`GroupKFold`), ROC AUC, balanced accuracy, permutation-based chance level, confusion matrices | Accuracy on a random epoch split; tuning on the test set |

An **optional extension** may compare one later method (for example, CSP from notebook 08) against your in-scope baseline. It is worth at most 5% bonus, and only if the in-scope analysis is already complete.

