## Choosing a challenging problem

A good project asks one testable question, phrased as a hypothesis, that the notebooks equip you to answer. “Classify motor imagery” is a task, not a question. “Does decoding transfer from executed to imagined movement?” is a question.

The ideas below are starting points; you may adapt them or invent your own. Ambition counts: the rubric awards 10% for project difficulty, so a well-executed Challenging project scores higher than a well-executed Standard one.

| Idea | Hypothesis to test | Datasets | Ambition |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Preprocessing ablation | Which of filtering, re-referencing, EOG regression and ICA actually changes decoding AUC, and by how much? | Any | Standard |
| How few channels? | P300 or MI performance stays within 5% of the full montage with a small, physiologically chosen subset | BNCI2014-009, EEGBCI | Standard |
| How many repetitions? | Character accuracy rises with flash repetitions; find the point where the gain stops being worth the time | BNCI2014-009, BNCI2015-003 | Standard |
| Executed versus imagined | A decoder trained on executed movement transfers to imagined movement in the same subjects | EEGBCI | Challenging |
| Session-to-session drift | Accuracy drops when training on day 1 and testing on day 2; band choice or normalization reduces the drop | BNCI2014-001, BNCI2014-004, Lee2019_MI | Challenging |
| Cross-subject generalization | A leave-one-subject-out decoder reaches chance-corrected performance; per-subject baselines show who it fails | Cho2017, EEGBCI | Challenging |
| BCI inefficiency | Resting or pre-trial alpha/mu power predicts which subjects decode well | Cho2017, Lee2019_MI | Challenging |
| Artifacts as confounds | Some “MI decoding” comes from eye or muscle activity; removing it changes which features win | BNCI2014-001 (has EOG) | Challenging |
| Clinical versus healthy P300 | ERP latency, amplitude and decoding differ between ALS and healthy users | BNCI2014-008 vs BNCI2014-009 | Challenging |

Avoid projects that only report one accuracy number, compare classifiers without a hypothesis, or need methods outside the scope table.

